To visit every cell, put a loop over columns inside a loop over rows. The inner loop runs fully for each row:

```javascript harness
const grid = [
  [1, 2, 3],
  [4, 5, 6],
];
{{code}}
console.log(`visited ${grid.length} rows of ${grid[0].length} cells`);
```

```javascript
for (let r = 0; r < grid.length; r++) {
  for (let c = 0; c < grid[r].length; c++) {
    // grid[r][c]
  }
}
```

Reset an accumulator in the outer loop to get one sum per row. For column sums, swap the loops: columns outside, rows inside, reading `grid[r][c]`.

When you only need the values, `for...of` reads cleaner: `for (const row of grid)` gives each row, and `for (const cell of row)` gives each value in it. That is the easy way to get a grand total.

On a square grid of size `n`, the main diagonal runs top-left to bottom-right, where the row equals the column: `grid[i][i]`. The anti-diagonal runs top-right to bottom-left: `grid[i][n - 1 - i]`. One loop over `i` covers both. Only square grids have these diagonals.

example.code:
const matrix = [
  [3, 1, 4],
  [1, 5, 9],
  [2, 6, 5],
];
const n = matrix.length;

for (let r = 0; r < n; r++) {
  let rowSum = 0;
  for (let c = 0; c < matrix[r].length; c++) {
    rowSum += matrix[r][c];
  }
  console.log(`Row ${r}: ${rowSum}`);
}

for (let c = 0; c < matrix[0].length; c++) {
  let colSum = 0;
  for (let r = 0; r < n; r++) {
    colSum += matrix[r][c];
  }
  console.log(`Col ${c}: ${colSum}`);
}

let total = 0;
for (const row of matrix) {
  for (const cell of row) {
    total += cell;
  }
}
console.log(`Total: ${total}`);

let mainSum = 0;
let antiSum = 0;
for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
  mainSum += matrix[i][i];
  antiSum += matrix[i][n - 1 - i];
}
console.log(`Main diagonal: ${mainSum}`);
console.log(`Anti-diagonal: ${antiSum}`);

