steps.body:
To build a grid of a given size, push a brand new row once per pass of a loop:

```javascript harness
const rows = 3;
const cols = 4;
{{code}}
console.log(JSON.stringify(grid));
```

```javascript
const grid = [];
for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
  grid.push(new Array(cols).fill(0));
}
```

**The gotcha:** `new Array(rows).fill([])` looks shorter but is wrong. `fill` puts the *same* array object in every slot, so `grid[0].push(7)` shows up in every row. `new Array(2).fill(new Array(2).fill(0))` has the same bug: the inner array is created once and shared. `grid[0] === grid[1]` is `true`, which proves it. Calling `new Array(cols)` inside the loop creates a fresh row each time.

`JSON.stringify(grid)` turns a whole grid into one line of text, like `[[0,0],[0,0]]`, so `console.log(JSON.stringify(grid))` shows every row at once.

To transpose, swap rows and columns: a rows by cols grid becomes a cols by rows grid where `t[c][r]` is `grid[r][c]`. Loop over columns outside, build each new row by pushing `grid[r][c]` for every `r`, and push that row. The original grid is untouched.

example.code:
const rows = 3;
const cols = 4;

const grid = [];
for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
  const row = new Array(cols).fill(0);
  for (let c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
    row[c] = r * c;
  }
  grid.push(row);
}
console.log(JSON.stringify(grid));

const shared = new Array(3).fill([]);
shared[0].push(7);
console.log(`Shared: ${JSON.stringify(shared)}`);
console.log(`Same row: ${shared[0] === shared[2]}`);

const transposed = [];
for (let c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
  const newRow = [];
  for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
    newRow.push(grid[r][c]);
  }
  transposed.push(newRow);
}
for (const row of transposed) {
  console.log(row.join(" "));
}

